    "result_text": "The full test suite passes: **272 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented digits-only telephone storage with normalization applied before the uniqueness check.\n\n**The key obstacle:** the OpenAPI-generated `OwnerFieldsDto` enforced `@Pattern(regexp = \"^[0-9]*$\")` on `telephone`. A formatted number like `(613) 555-0100` would have been rejected with a 400 at bind time, never reaching storage. So the pattern had to be relaxed to accept the punctuation, then strip it during processing.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 relaxed the telephone `pattern` from `^[0-9]*$` to `^[0-9 ()+.-]*$` so formatted input passes validation (the DTO is regenerated during the build's `generate-sources` phase). Updated the field description accordingly.\n\n2. **`Telephones.java`** (new) \u2014 a small package-private helper with `digitsOnly(String)` that strips all non-digit characters (null-safe).\n\n3. **`BuildOwner.java`** (POST path) \u2014 normalizes the telephone to digits-only right after mapping the request. This runs *before* `CheckDuplicateOwner` in the POST pipeline, so both storage and the uniqueness comparison see digits only \u2014 `(613) 555-0100` and `6135550100` collide.\n\n4. **`ApplyOwner.java`** (PUT path) \u2014 applies the same normalization so updates also store digits only.\n\nSince the stored value is digits-only, the response `telephone` field (built from the entity) returns digits, satisfying the \"return the stored value\" requirement. The existing `CheckDuplicateOwner`'s `DuplicateKey.normalize` still works correctly because it operates on already-normalized (digit-only) values.\n\nBoth new cp11 tests pass (`coreStoresDigitsOnly`, `functionalityNormalisedDuplicateRejected`), and all pre-existing tests remain green.",
